  "summary": "The second critique correctly identifies that MAD_DIR doesn't influence Config() resolution, but its concern about a WorkingDirectory race condition is unfounded — systemd guarantees cwd is set before ExecStart runs, so find_config() will always see the correct cwd. The real gap is subtler: find_config() lines 64-66 track 'found_local' when ANY .mad/ directory exists in the parent tree (even without a config.json inside it), which prevents the None return at line 73 that would trigger ensure_local_config(). Instead it falls through to the global fallback at line 76. This means a project at ~/chickentracks.app that has a .mad/ directory (e.g., created by a previous partial setup) but no .mad/config.json will silently inherit ~/MAD/config.json and its lock namespace. The correct fix is option (a) from the second critique — have install_service() call ensure_local_config(project_dir) — but with one addition: find_config() should also be hardened so that when a .mad/ directory exists without a config.json, it creates the config rather than falling back to global. Specifically, the 'found_local' branch (lines 71-73) should call ensure_local_config(dir_with_mad) instead of returning None, since the presence of .mad/ signals project intent. Option (b) (hash-based lock files) is over-engineering that treats the symptom rather than the disease — the real problem is config resolution, and lock isolation follows naturally from correct per-project configs. The fix is two lines: (1) in install_service(), add ensure_local_config(project_dir) before starting the service, and (2) in find_config(), when found_local is True but no config.json was found, return ensure_local_config() for that directory instead of falling through to global."
